# Template: add a bespoke tool - beyond the generic query_data/list_tables
# pair - bound to an existing connection, when the agent needs a
# specific, named, parameterized operation rather than free-form SQL.
# Good for: a query you want the agent to run consistently the same way
# (fixed joins/filters), or one you want to expose without also exposing
# free-form query_data on that source.
#
# HOW TO USE:
#   The easiest way is to just ask Claude Code, e.g. "add a toolbox tool
#   that counts active users in the primary Postgres DB" - it will follow
#   ../validate.sh's snapshot/check/restore flow (see that script and the
#   "Adding or editing a connection safely" section of ../README.md), so a
#   mistake here can't take down every other connection along with it. To
#   do it by hand:
#   1. Find the live directory: `claude mcp list | grep
#      '^plugin:agentic-development-kit:toolbox'` shows the exact
#      --config-folder path.
#   2. `../validate.sh snapshot <that path>` - saves the current working
#      state so a mistake below is recoverable.
#   3. Copy this file into that directory (its own file, or appended into
#      an existing connection's file - Toolbox merges every *.yaml/*.yml in
#      that directory into one config either way).
#   4. Point `source:` at an existing source name from one of the files
#      already there (e.g. `postgres-primary-source`).
#   5. Rename the tool and rewrite `statement`/`parameters` for your case.
#   6. Add it to that connection's toolset (or a new one) if you want it
#      grouped there - grouping is informational only for stdio, every
#      `kind: tool` block is exposed to Claude Code regardless of toolset.
#   7. `../validate.sh check <that path>` - confirms toolbox can actually
#      start with the new tool. If it FAILS, run `../validate.sh restore
#      <that path>` to undo, fix the error it printed, and try again from
#      step 3.

kind: tool
name: postgres_primary_active_users_last_n_days
type: postgres-execute-sql
source: postgres-primary-source  # must match a source already defined in that directory
description: >-
  Count users in the primary PostgreSQL database who logged in within the
  last N days. Use this instead of query_data when you specifically need
  this metric - it's faster to reach for and always uses the same
  definition of "active".
statement: |
  SELECT count(*) AS active_users
  FROM users
  WHERE last_login_at >= now() - ($1 || ' days')::interval;
parameters:
  - name: days
    type: integer
    description: How many days back to count logins from (e.g. 7, 30).
annotations:
  readOnlyHint: true
  destructiveHint: false
